## 1\. APDS\-9960 gesture sensor to ESP32

Suggested connections for a typical ESP32 DevKit and APDS\-9960 breakout:

|APDS-9960 pin|ESP32 connection                             |
|-------------|---------------------------------------------|
|VIN / VCC    |3V3, only if the breakout supports 3.3V input|
|GND          |GND                                          |
|SDA          |GPIO 21                                      |
|SCL          |GPIO 22                                      |
|INT          |Leave unconnected for the initial test       |

Check the exact breakout board documentation\. Some APDS\-9960 boards have different labels or voltage\-regulator/level\-shifter arrangements\. Do not assume every board’s VIN pin accepts the same voltage\. Use 3\.3V logic with the ESP32\.

Mount the sensor facing outward, with a clear view of the hand\. Avoid direct sunlight and reflective surfaces during initial testing\. The sensor detects directional swipes within a limited range; it is not a camera and does not identify a fist reliably\.

## 2\. ESP32 to L298N motor driver

Suggested GPIO assignments\. Confirm the exact ESP32 board pin labels\.

|L298N pin|ESP32 connection|Purpose              |
|---------|----------------|---------------------|
|ENA      |GPIO 25 (PWM)   |Left motor speed     |
|IN1      |GPIO 27         |Left motor direction |
|IN2      |GPIO 14         |Left motor direction |
|ENB      |GPIO 26 (PWM)   |Right motor speed    |
|IN3      |GPIO 18         |Right motor direction|
|IN4      |GPIO 19         |Right motor direction|
|GND      |ESP32 GND       |Common ground        |

If the module has ENA/ENB jumper caps, check its documentation before using PWM on those pins\.

## 3\. Motors

|L298N output |Connect to    |
|-------------|--------------|
|OUT1 and OUT2|Left DC motor |
|OUT3 and OUT4|Right DC motor|

If a motor turns opposite to the intended direction, correct the direction logic in software or swap that motor’s two output wires while power is disconnected\.

## 4\. Power and safety

1. Power the ESP32 through its USB connector using a suitable USB supply\.
2. Connect the motor battery to the L298N motor supply input, observing polarity and the module’s voltage requirements\.
3. Connect ESP32 GND, APDS\-9960 GND, and L298N GND together\.
4. Never connect motor battery positive to the ESP32 3V3 pin\.
5. Never power the motors directly from the ESP32\.
6. Confirm the motors’ rated voltage and current, battery voltage, and L298N module limits before selecting or connecting the battery\. A 4xAA pack is not confirmed suitable until those ratings are known\.
7. Disconnect all power before changing wiring\.
8. Test with the wheels raised off the ground, at low speed, in a clear area\.

## 5\. Intended gesture behaviour

|APDS-9960 gesture event         |Intended action   |
|--------------------------------|------------------|
|UP                              |Forward briefly   |
|DOWN                            |Reverse briefly   |
|LEFT                            |Turn left briefly |
|RIGHT                           |Turn right briefly|
|No new gesture / command timeout|Stop              |

The APDS\-9960 recognises directional swipes, not a dependable closed\-fist command\. The controller should stop automatically after a short movement interval unless a new valid gesture is received\. A separate physical power switch should remain accessible\.

## 6\. Bring\-up and test order

1. Identify the exact ESP32, APDS\-9960 breakout, L298N module, motors, and battery\.
2. Test the APDS\-9960 alone with the ESP32 over I2C\.
3. Confirm that UP, DOWN, LEFT, and RIGHT events are read correctly\.
4. Test the L298N and each motor separately with wheels raised\.
5. Implement short movement pulses and an automatic stop timeout\.
6. Test the full car slowly in a clear area\.

## Status

This is a preliminary wiring plan, not a verified schematic\. Exact modules and ratings have not yet been inspected\. Do not connect the battery or assemble the circuit until the component labels and electrical ratings have been checked\.
